IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tasap-dates`

-- Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas), s 27 -- rights of action by and against personal representatives:
-- survival of causes of action on death.
--
-- SEAT: the individual whose claim a death touches. Either (a) the personal representative (executor or administrator)
-- or family of a person who has died, bringing or continuing that person's claim for the benefit of the estate ("Does the
-- claim survive?", "What can the estate recover?", "Does a dust-disease claim keep its damages for pain and
-- suffering?"), or (b) a person with a claim against someone who has died ("Does my claim survive their death?", "Can I
-- still sue the estate?", "By when must I take proceedings?"). s 27(7) and (8) are asked by the person who owes rent or
-- a rent-charge to someone who has died ("Can the executor distrain on me?"). The judge gets no seat: what a judge would
-- allow is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline (taking proceedings in tort against the estate of a dead wrongdoer not later than
-- 6 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration, s 27(5)(b)) is a regulative rule run inside a
-- question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole of s 27: (1) survival; (2) omitted (noted); (3) damages for the estate; (3A) to (3C) the
-- dust-related condition rule; (4) a person liable who dies before or at the time of the damage; (5) and (6) conditions
-- and time for proceedings in tort against an estate; (7) and (8) distress by a personal representative; (9) the Fatal
-- Accidents Act 1934 (claims against estates brought within s 27; the rest a saving).
--
-- Edition: Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas), No. 38 of 1935, consolidated version published 2024-07-18 and
-- current at point in time 2026-11-06, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-1935-038.txt. No regulation sets a
-- number s 27 needs.
--
-- (c) State of Tasmania. Quoted under CC BY 4.0 from an unofficial plain-text copy (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Based on
-- material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a judge are INPUTS. Answers follow the
-- rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the
-- text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording and says nothing more is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `s 27 -- Survival of causes of action: the questions of the estate and of a person with a claim against an estate`

-- s 27(2) was omitted by No. 43 of 2006, s 4, "Applied: 18 Dec 2006", which also amended s 27(1). The text of the
-- former s 27(2) is not deposited, and it may have excepted some causes of action from survival. A death on or after
-- 18 December 2006 is answered under the text as it now stands; an earlier death is not answered (fork F1, finding 1).
`the day s 27(2) was omitted` MEANS `the calendar date` 18 12 2006

-- s 27(3A) to (3C) were inserted by No. 76 of 2005, s 4, "Applied: 15 Dec 2005": "the commencement of that subsection"
-- in s 27(3C).
`the commencement of s 27(3A)` MEANS `the calendar date` 15 12 2005

§§ `s 27(1) and (4) -- Does the cause of action survive the death`

-- s 27(1): "Subject to the provisions of this section on the death of any person after the commencement of this section
-- all causes of action -- (a) subsisting against him shall survive against his estate; and (b) vested in him shall
-- survive for the benefit of his estate."
-- s 27(4): "Where damage has been suffered by reason of any act or omission in respect of which a cause of action would
-- have subsisted against any person if that person had not died before, or at the same time as, the damage was
-- suffered, the cause of action which would have subsisted against him if he had died after the damage was suffered,
-- shall be deemed to have subsisted against him before he died."
-- No kind of cause of action is excepted in the text as it now stands. A cause of action that had not arisen at the
-- death (and is not saved by s 27(4)) is outside the text's own words "subsisting against" or "vested in" (NOTHING-kind,
-- fork F2).
DECLARE `Which way the claim runs` IS ONE OF
    `a claim the dead person had against someone else (it would benefit the estate)`
    `a claim someone has against the dead person (it would be brought against the estate)`

DECLARE `Does the claim survive the death` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it survives for the benefit of the dead person's estate (s 27(1)(b)); the personal representative may bring or continue it, with damages limited by s 27(3)`
    `yes: it survives against the dead person's estate (s 27(1)(a)); proceedings in tort must meet s 27(5)`
    `yes: s 27(4) deems it to have existed against the person before they died, so it survives against the estate (s 27(1)(a), s 27(4)); proceedings in tort must meet s 27(5)`
    `not answered: the death was before 18 December 2006, when the former s 27(2) (not deposited) was omitted; that text may have excepted this kind of claim (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 27 does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does a claim survive the death of the person who had it, or of the person it was against (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(1) and (4))? On a death on or after 18 December 2006, every cause of action the person had survives for the benefit of their estate, and every cause of action against them survives against their estate, including one for damage suffered only at or after their death.
GIVEN `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `which way` IS A `Which way the claim runs` @desc Whether the claim is one the dead person had (for their estate) or one against the dead person (against their estate)
      `existed at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of action had arisen by the time of the death (everything needed to sue had happened, including the damage)
      `damage only at or after the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a claim against the dead person: whether the damage came from their act or omission but was suffered only at or after their death (answer no for a claim the dead person had)
GIVETH A `Does the claim survive the death`
`s 27(1) -- does the claim survive the death` `died on` `which way` `existed at the death` `damage only at or after the death` MEANS
    IF `died on` `falls before` `the day s 27(2) was omitted`
    THEN `not answered: the death was before 18 December 2006, when the former s 27(2) (not deposited) was omitted; that text may have excepted this kind of claim (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `which way` EQUALS `a claim the dead person had against someone else (it would benefit the estate)`
         THEN IF `existed at the death`
              THEN `yes: it survives for the benefit of the dead person's estate (s 27(1)(b)); the personal representative may bring or continue it, with damages limited by s 27(3)`
              ELSE `s 27 does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `existed at the death`
              THEN `yes: it survives against the dead person's estate (s 27(1)(a)); proceedings in tort must meet s 27(5)`
              ELSE IF `damage only at or after the death`
                   THEN `yes: s 27(4) deems it to have existed against the person before they died, so it survives against the estate (s 27(1)(a), s 27(4)); proceedings in tort must meet s 27(5)`
                   ELSE `s 27 does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 27(3A) to (3C) -- Dust-related condition: damages for pain and suffering after death`

-- s 27(3)(c)(ii): where the death was caused by the act or omission giving rise to the cause of action, the estate's
-- damages "shall not include damages for pain or suffering, for any bodily or mental harm suffered by him or for the
-- curtailment of his expectation of life".
-- s 27(3A): "Notwithstanding subsection (3)(c), damages for pain and suffering, for any bodily or mental harm or for
-- curtailment of expectation of life, may be recovered if -- (a) the cause of action related to a dust-related
-- condition; and (b) the deceased person commenced a proceeding in relation to the cause of action before the deceased
-- person died; and (c) the deceased person died as a result of the dust-related condition or the dust-related condition
-- was a contributing factor to the deceased person's death."
-- s 27(3B): "dust-related condition means -- (a) any of the following diseases: [fourteen named]; or (b) any other
-- pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust." A "means" definition: when no
-- limb holds, the text's no.
-- s 27(3C): s 27(3A) "applies in relation to a proceeding ... whether the proceeding commenced before or after the
-- commencement of that subsection unless -- (a) judgment has been given in relation to the proceeding; or (b) the
-- proceeding has been settled or withdrawn." Read as judgment, settlement or withdrawal before 15 December 2005 (fork
-- F4, finding 3). "unless" met is the text's no.
-- s 27(3A) lifts only the (3)(c)(ii) exclusion: (3)(c)(i) and (iii) (losses from the death, earnings after the death)
-- still apply.

DECLARE `The condition` IS ONE OF
    `aluminosis`
    `asbestosis`
    `asbestos-induced carcinoma`
    `asbestos-related pleural disease`
    `bagassosis`
    `berylliosis`
    `byssinosis`
    `coal dust pneumoconiosis`
    `farmers' lung`
    `hard metal pneumoconiosis`
    `mesothelioma`
    `silicosis`
    `silico-tuberculosis`
    `talcosis`
    `another pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum`
    `another condition, or no condition of this kind`

-- s 27(3B): a named disease, or another pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust.
GIVEN `the condition` IS A `The condition`, `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a dust-related condition` `the condition` `attributable to dust` IF
        (`the named disease` OR `another lung condition from dust`)
    WHERE
        `another lung condition from dust` MEANS
            (`the condition` EQUALS `another pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum`) AND `attributable to dust`
        `the named disease` MEANS
                (NOT (`the condition` EQUALS `another pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum`))
            AND (NOT (`the condition` EQUALS `another condition, or no condition of this kind`))

-- s 27(3C): the proceeding was ended by judgment, settlement or withdrawal before 15 December 2005 (fork F4).
GIVEN `ended` IS A BOOLEAN, `ended on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ended before s 27(3A) commenced` `ended` `ended on` IF
    `ended` AND (`ended on` `falls before` `the commencement of s 27(3A)`)

DECLARE `Does the dust rule keep damages for pain and suffering` IS ONE OF
    `the rule is not needed: the death was not caused by the act or omission the claim is about, so s 27(3)(c) does not take those damages out`
    `yes: the estate may recover damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 27(3A))`
    `no: the condition is not a dust-related condition (s 27(3B), the text's no); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`
    `no: s 27(3A) needs the person to have commenced a proceeding on the claim before they died, and to have died as a result of the condition or with it a contributing factor (the text's no: its "if" fails); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`
    `no: judgment had been given in the proceeding, or it had been settled or withdrawn, before 15 December 2005 (s 27(3C), the text's no); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`

GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN, `the condition` IS A `The condition`, `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN, `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN, `died of it or it contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `ended` IS A BOOLEAN, `ended on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Does the dust rule keep damages for pain and suffering`
`the s 27(3A) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of it or it contributed` `ended` `ended on` MEANS
    IF NOT `death caused by the wrong`
    THEN `the rule is not needed: the death was not caused by the act or omission the claim is about, so s 27(3)(c) does not take those damages out`
    ELSE IF NOT (`a dust-related condition` `the condition` `attributable to dust`)
         THEN `no: the condition is not a dust-related condition (s 27(3B), the text's no); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`
         ELSE IF NOT (`commenced before death` AND `died of it or it contributed`)
              THEN `no: s 27(3A) needs the person to have commenced a proceeding on the claim before they died, and to have died as a result of the condition or with it a contributing factor (the text's no: its "if" fails); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`
              ELSE IF `ended before s 27(3A) commenced` `ended` `ended on`
                   THEN `no: judgment had been given in the proceeding, or it had been settled or withdrawn, before 15 December 2005 (s 27(3C), the text's no); s 27(3)(c)(ii) takes those damages out`
                   ELSE `yes: the estate may recover damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 27(3A))`

@export If a person with a dust-related condition (asbestosis, mesothelioma, silicosis and the like) dies after starting a claim for it, can their estate still recover damages for their pain and suffering (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(3A) to (3C))? Yes, if they commenced the proceeding before they died and the condition caused or contributed to their death.
GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about (for example, the exposure to dust)
      `the condition` IS A `The condition` @desc The condition the claim is about: one of the fourteen diseases the Act names, another pathological condition of the lung, pleura (lining of the lungs) or peritoneum (lining of the abdomen), or something else
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum: whether it is attributable to dust (for the named diseases this is not used)
      `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had commenced a proceeding on the claim before they died
      `died of it or it contributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died as a result of the condition, or the condition was a contributing factor to their death
      `ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether judgment has been given in the proceeding, or it has been settled or withdrawn
      `ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day judgment was given or the proceeding was settled or withdrawn (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Does the dust rule keep damages for pain and suffering`
`s 27(3A) -- does a dust-disease claim keep its damages for pain and suffering` `death caused by the wrong` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of it or it contributed` `ended` `ended on` MEANS
    `the s 27(3A) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of it or it contributed` `ended` `ended on`

§§ `s 27(3) -- Damages the estate can recover`

-- s 27(3): where a cause of action survives for the benefit of the estate, the damages recoverable for the estate
--   (a) shall not include exemplary damages;
--   (b) in the case of a breach of promise to marry, shall be limited to such damage (if any) to that estate as flows
--       from the breach of promise to marry; and
--   (c) where the death of that person has been caused by the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of action:
--       (i) shall be calculated without reference to any loss or gain to that estate consequent on his death, except
--           that a sum in respect of funeral expenses may be included;
--       (ii) shall not include damages for pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, or curtailment of expectation of
--           life (subject to s 27(3A));
--       (iii) shall be calculated without reference to the future probable earnings of the deceased if he had survived,
--           and without any allowance for loss of earning capacity relating to a period after his death.
-- The amounts are a court's assessment apart from s 27(3), asked as facts; s 27(3) only takes heads out. "a sum in
-- respect of funeral expenses may be included" is read as letting the estate recover the funeral expenses assessed
-- (fork F5). A gain to the estate consequent on the death (life insurance, for example) is not deducted: it is "without
-- reference to" it, so it is not asked. s 27(9): these rights are in addition to the dependants' rights under the
-- Fatal Accidents Act 1934, which this encoding does not reach.

@export How much can the estate recover on a claim that survived the death (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(3) and (3A))? Never exemplary damages; for a breach of promise to marry, only the damage to the estate; and where the wrong caused the death, no damages for pain and suffering (unless the dust-related condition rule applies), for earnings after death or for loss to the estate from the death, but funeral expenses may be included. Assumes the claim survives (see s 27(1)).
GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `breach of promise to marry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for breach of a promise to marry
      `damage to the estate from the breach` IS A NUMBER @desc For a breach of promise to marry: the damage (in dollars) to the estate itself that flows from the breach; otherwise 0
      `damages up to death` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) a court would assess for the dead person's losses not listed below (for example medical costs, lost earnings up to death, damaged property)
      `non-economic damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) a court would assess for the dead person's pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life
      `earnings after death` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) a court would assess for the earnings the person would probably have made, or the earning capacity lost, for the period after the death
      `loss from the death` IS A NUMBER @desc Any other loss (in dollars) to the estate that results from the death itself, other than funeral expenses
      `funeral expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc The funeral expenses (in dollars)
      `exemplary damages` IS A NUMBER @desc Any exemplary (punitive) damages (in dollars) a court would otherwise award
      `the condition` IS A `The condition` @desc The condition the claim is about, if it is a disease of the lungs or their linings (otherwise "another condition, or no condition of this kind")
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum: whether it is attributable to dust
      `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had commenced a proceeding on the claim before they died
      `died of it or it contributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died as a result of that condition, or it was a contributing factor to their death
      `ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether judgment has been given in the proceeding, or it has been settled or withdrawn
      `ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day judgment was given or the proceeding was settled or withdrawn (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 27(3) -- how much can the estate recover` `death caused by the wrong` `breach of promise to marry` `damage to the estate from the breach` `damages up to death` `non-economic damages` `earnings after death` `loss from the death` `funeral expenses` `exemplary damages` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of it or it contributed` `ended` `ended on` MEANS
    IF `breach of promise to marry`
    THEN `damage to the estate from the breach`
    ELSE IF `death caused by the wrong`
         THEN `damages up to death` PLUS `funeral expenses` PLUS `pain and suffering kept`
         ELSE `damages up to death` PLUS `non-economic damages` PLUS `earnings after death` PLUS `loss from the death` PLUS `funeral expenses`
    WHERE
        `pain and suffering kept` MEANS
            IF `the s 27(3A) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `the condition` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of it or it contributed` `ended` `ended on`
                EQUALS `yes: the estate may recover damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 27(3A))`
            THEN `non-economic damages`
            ELSE 0

§§ `s 27(5), (6) and (9) -- Proceedings in tort against the estate of a person who has died`

-- s 27(5): "No proceedings shall be maintainable in respect of a cause of action in tort which by virtue of this section
-- has survived against the estate of a deceased person unless -- (a) proceedings against him in respect of that cause
-- of action were pending at the date of his death; or (b) the cause of action arose not earlier than 12 months before
-- the date of his death and proceedings are taken not later than 6 months, or such extended time as a judge may allow,
-- after the date of the grant of probate of his will, or letters of administration, to his personal representative."
-- s 27(6): "A judge, on application, may extend the period of 6 months ... and such application may be made although
-- the said period of 6 months has expired."
-- s 27(9): "so much of this Act as relates to causes of action against the estates of deceased persons shall apply in
-- relation to causes of action under the [Fatal Accidents Act 1934]": s 27(5) reaches those claims too (fork F6).
-- "unless" failing is the text's no. A cause of action not in tort (and not under that Act) is outside s 27(5), which
-- says nothing about it (NOTHING-kind, fork F6). Unlike some other States' versions, nothing lets a court relax the
-- "12 months before the death" limb (finding 2).

DECLARE `The kind of claim against the estate` IS ONE OF
    `a claim in tort (negligence, trespass, nuisance and so on)`
    `a claim under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934`
    `another kind of claim (contract, debt and so on)`

DECLARE `Person with a claim against an estate` IS ONE OF `the person with the claim`
DECLARE `Step against the estate` IS ONE OF `take proceedings against the estate`

-- The duty with a deadline, counted in days from the grant of probate or administration to the last day (6 months
-- after the grant, or the end of the extended time a judge allows).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Person with a claim against an estate` `Step against the estate`
`s 27(5)(b) -- take proceedings within 6 months after the grant` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person with the claim`
    MUST `take proceedings against the estate`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person with the claim` BECAUSE "proceedings in tort against the estate not taken within 6 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration, or the extended time a judge allowed (s 27(5)(b))"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. FULFILLED means in time. It takes no DEONTIC from outside and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 27(5) -- taking proceedings on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 27(5)(b) -- take proceedings within 6 months after the grant` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person with the claim` `take proceedings against the estate` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The last day: 6 months after the grant, or the end of the extended time a judge has allowed, whichever is later (an
-- extension extends; fork F8).
GIVEN `granted on` IS A DATE, `extended` IS A BOOLEAN, `extension ends on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 27(5) last day` `granted on` `extended` `extension ends on` MEANS
    IF `extended` AND ((`months after` 6 `granted on`) `falls before` `extension ends on`)
    THEN `extension ends on`
    ELSE `months after` 6 `granted on`

-- "arose not earlier than 12 months before the date of his death".
GIVEN `arose on` IS A DATE, `died on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `arose within the 12 months before the death` `arose on` `died on` IF
    NOT (`arose on` `falls before` (`months before` 12 `died on`))

@export By when must I take proceedings in tort against the estate of the person who wronged me, now that they have died (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(5)(b) and (6))? Not later than 6 months after probate or letters of administration are granted to their personal representative, or any extended time a judge allows. No date until the grant is made.
GIVEN `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the dead person's will, or letters of administration of their estate, have been granted to their personal representative
      `granted on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a judge has extended the 6 months for you
      `extension ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the extended time the judge allowed (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 27(5) -- by when must I take proceedings against the estate` `granted` `granted on` `extended` `extension ends on` MEANS
    IF `granted`
    THEN JUST (`the s 27(5) last day` `granted on` `extended` `extension ends on`)
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Can I take proceedings against the estate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: proceedings against the person were pending when they died (s 27(5)(a))`
    `yes: you took proceedings in time, not later than 6 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration, or the extended time a judge allowed (s 27(5)(b))`
    `still in time: you have not yet taken proceedings, and the last day (6 months after the grant, or the extended time allowed) has not passed (s 27(5)(b))`
    `still in time: probate or letters of administration have not been granted, so the 6 months has not begun (s 27(5)(b))`
    `out of time (s 27(5)(b), the text's no): a judge may still extend the 6 months, on an application made even after it has expired (s 27(6))`
    `no: the cause of action arose earlier than 12 months before the death, and no proceedings were pending when the person died (s 27(5), the text's no)`
    `s 27(5) sets no condition: the claim is not in tort or under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (NOTHING-kind); the ordinary limitation law still applies`

@export Can I still bring or continue a claim in tort against the estate of the person who wronged me, now that they have died (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(5), (6) and (9))? Only if proceedings against them were pending when they died, or the cause of action arose no earlier than 12 months before the death and proceedings are taken not later than 6 months after probate or letters of administration are granted (or any extended time a judge allows). Claims under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 are treated the same way. Assumes the claim survives (see s 27(1)).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `The kind of claim against the estate` @desc What kind of claim you have against the dead person
      `pending at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had proceedings on this claim against the person under way when they died
      `arose on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (usually the day you suffered the damage)
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate of the dead person's will, or letters of administration of their estate, have been granted to their personal representative
      `granted on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were granted (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a judge has extended the 6 months for you
      `extension ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the extended time the judge allowed (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have taken proceedings against the estate on the claim
      `taken on` IS A DATE @desc The day you took them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Can I take proceedings against the estate`
`s 27(5) -- can I take proceedings against the estate` `the kind` `pending at death` `arose on` `died on` `granted` `granted on` `extended` `extension ends on` `taken` `taken on` `today` MEANS
    IF `the kind` EQUALS `another kind of claim (contract, debt and so on)`
    THEN `s 27(5) sets no condition: the claim is not in tort or under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (NOTHING-kind); the ordinary limitation law still applies`
    ELSE IF `pending at death`
         THEN `yes: proceedings against the person were pending when they died (s 27(5)(a))`
         ELSE IF NOT (`arose within the 12 months before the death` `arose on` `died on`)
              THEN `no: the cause of action arose earlier than 12 months before the death, and no proceedings were pending when the person died (s 27(5), the text's no)`
              ELSE IF NOT `granted`
                   THEN IF `taken`
                        THEN `yes: you took proceedings in time, not later than 6 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration, or the extended time a judge allowed (s 27(5)(b))`
                        ELSE `still in time: probate or letters of administration have not been granted, so the 6 months has not begun (s 27(5)(b))`
                   ELSE IF `taken`
                        THEN IF `s 27(5) -- taking proceedings on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day taken`
                             THEN `yes: you took proceedings in time, not later than 6 months after the grant of probate or letters of administration, or the extended time a judge allowed (s 27(5)(b))`
                             ELSE `out of time (s 27(5)(b), the text's no): a judge may still extend the 6 months, on an application made even after it has expired (s 27(6))`
                        ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `today`
                             THEN `out of time (s 27(5)(b), the text's no): a judge may still extend the 6 months, on an application made even after it has expired (s 27(6))`
                             ELSE `still in time: you have not yet taken proceedings, and the last day (6 months after the grant, or the extended time allowed) has not passed (s 27(5)(b))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the s 27(5) last day` `granted on` `extended` `extension ends on`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `granted on` `to` `the last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `granted on` `to` `taken on`
        -- Proceedings taken before the grant are counted as taken on day 0 (in time). Fork F9.
        `the day taken` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `s 27(7) and (8) -- Distress by the personal representative (for a person who owed rent or a rent-charge to someone who has died)`

-- Situation: only arises if you owed rent, or a rent-charge on your land, to a person who has died.
-- s 27(7): "A personal representative may distrain for arrears of a rent-charge due or accruing to the deceased in his
-- lifetime on the land affected or charged therewith, so long as the land remains in the possession of the person
-- liable to pay the rent-charge or of the persons deriving title under him, and in like manner as the deceased might
-- have done had he been living." "so long as" failing is the text's no.
-- s 27(8): "A personal representative may distrain upon land for arrears of rent due or accruing to the deceased in like
-- manner as the deceased might have done had he been living, and such arrears may be distrained for after the
-- termination of the lease or tenancy as if the term or interest had not determined, if the distress is made -- (a)
-- within 6 months after the termination of the lease or tenancy; and (b) during the continuance of the possession of
-- the lessee or tenant from whom the arrears were due". The "if" failing is the text's no.
-- Arrears not due or accruing to the deceased are outside both subsections (NOTHING-kind). Whether another law now
-- limits distress for rent was not checked (finding 6).

DECLARE `Can the personal representative distrain for the rent-charge` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the personal representative may distrain on the land for the arrears, as the dead person could have (s 27(7))`
    `no: the land is no longer in the possession of the person liable to pay the rent-charge or of those deriving title under them (s 27(7), the text's no)`
    `s 27(7) does not reach it: the arrears were not due or accruing to the dead person in their lifetime (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can the executor or administrator of a person who has died distrain on my land for arrears of a rent-charge I owed that person (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(7))? Yes, so long as the land remains in the possession of the person liable to pay the rent-charge or of those deriving title under them.
GIVEN `arrears due to the deceased` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the arrears of the rent-charge were due or accruing to the person who died, in their lifetime
      `still in possession of the person liable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the land is still in the possession of the person liable to pay the rent-charge, or of a person deriving title under them (for example, you as their successor)
GIVETH A `Can the personal representative distrain for the rent-charge`
`s 27(7) -- can the executor distrain for the rent-charge` `arrears due to the deceased` `still in possession of the person liable` MEANS
    IF NOT `arrears due to the deceased`
    THEN `s 27(7) does not reach it: the arrears were not due or accruing to the dead person in their lifetime (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `still in possession of the person liable`
         THEN `yes: the personal representative may distrain on the land for the arrears, as the dead person could have (s 27(7))`
         ELSE `no: the land is no longer in the possession of the person liable to pay the rent-charge or of those deriving title under them (s 27(7), the text's no)`

DECLARE `Can the personal representative distrain for the rent` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the lease or tenancy has not ended, and the personal representative may distrain for the arrears as the dead person could have (s 27(8))`
    `yes: the lease or tenancy has ended, but the distress is within 6 months after it ended and you are still in possession (s 27(8)(a) and (b))`
    `no: after the lease or tenancy ended, distress is allowed only within 6 months after it ended and while the tenant remains in possession (s 27(8), the text's no)`
    `s 27(8) does not reach it: the arrears were not due or accruing to the dead person (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can the executor or administrator of my landlord, who has died, distrain on the land for rent I owed the landlord (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 27(8))? Yes, as the landlord could have; after the lease or tenancy has ended, only within 6 months after it ended and while you remain in possession.
GIVEN `arrears due to the deceased` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the arrears of rent were due or accruing to the landlord who died
      `ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the lease or tenancy has ended
      `ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day the lease or tenancy ended (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `distress on` IS A DATE @desc The day the distress is (or would be) made
      `still in possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the tenant from whom the arrears were due) are still in possession on that day
GIVETH A `Can the personal representative distrain for the rent`
`s 27(8) -- can the executor distrain for the rent` `arrears due to the deceased` `ended` `ended on` `distress on` `still in possession` MEANS
    IF NOT `arrears due to the deceased`
    THEN `s 27(8) does not reach it: the arrears were not due or accruing to the dead person (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `ended`
         THEN `yes: the lease or tenancy has not ended, and the personal representative may distrain for the arrears as the dead person could have (s 27(8))`
         ELSE IF (NOT ((`months after` 6 `ended on`) `falls before` `distress on`)) AND `still in possession`
              THEN `yes: the lease or tenancy has ended, but the distress is within 6 months after it ended and you are still in possession (s 27(8)(a) and (b))`
              ELSE `no: after the lease or tenancy ended, distress is allowed only within 6 months after it ended and while the tenant remains in possession (s 27(8), the text's no)`
